# On the VPS this lives at /etc/fernscout/env and is read by systemd
# (EnvironmentFile= in deploy/fernscout.service). Locally, copy it to .env.
# and fill in what your deployment tier needs. Every variable here is
# optional in the sense that the app boots without it — but a capability that
# needs one and doesn't have it reports itself disabled at /api/health rather
# than pretending to work. See lib/capabilities.ts and docs/runbook.md.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Core — every tier
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The public URL this instance is served at. Used for absolute links,
# canonical tags, and push notification URLs. Falls back to site/config.json's
# site.url if unset.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://example.com

# Where the file store (reactions, push subscriptions) and, once the data
# layer lands, a SQLite database file live. MUST be outside the repo/image so
# a rebuild or `git pull` can never wipe it. On the VPS this is
# a named volume at /data and points DATA_DIR there.
DATA_DIR=/data

# Where the journals live — every person's config.json, trip and photograph,
# and nothing else (B510). Unset means `<repo>/content`, which is what local
# development wants: the example journal is committed, so a fresh clone
# renders.
#
# On a server, point this OUTSIDE the repository. `scripts/deploy.sh` runs
# `git pull --ff-only`, and an agent writing a draft over the API writes into
# that same working tree — the next deploy then fails on local modifications.
# The dictionaries, the rates and the imprint are unaffected either way: they
# live in `site/` in the checkout and arrive with the pull.
# CONTENT_DIR=/var/lib/fernscout/content

# This instance's own server config — site name, URL, default user, which
# capabilities the server is able to offer at all.
#
# Unset means `site/config.json` in the checkout, which is the shipped default
# a fresh clone runs on. On a server, point this at a file OUTSIDE the
# repository: it switches on capabilities (mail, auth) whose credentials only
# exist on this machine, so committing it would make every fork fail its boot
# check. Secrets themselves never go in it — they go here, in this file.
# FERNSCOUT_CONFIG=/var/lib/fernscout/config.json

# Where this instance's own four files live — the shipped config, the UI
# dictionaries, the currency reference rates and the imprint (B510). Unset
# means `site/` in the checkout, which is what a `git pull` updates and what
# almost every install wants. Override it only for a packaging layout that
# puts them somewhere else; it is not where journals go (that is CONTENT_DIR).
# SITE_DIR=/opt/fernscout/site

# Caddy's ACME account and the domain(s) it requests certificates for.
# CADDY_DOMAIN can be a bare domain ("fernscout.ch") or include a port for
# local testing ("localhost:443" — see Caddyfile). A comma-separated list of
# domains also works.
CADDY_DOMAIN=example.com
# Let's Encrypt sends renewal/expiry notices here. Required by Caddy's ACME
# client in production.
CADDY_ACME_EMAIL=admin@example.com

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database — prototype tier leaves this unset entirely (ROADMAP §2.2).
# Install Postgres on the VPS and point this at it;
# self-hosters may instead point it at any Postgres they already run.
# Local development uses `sqlite:./…` and needs no database server at all —
# see docs/plans/W06-data-layer.md.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DATABASE_URL=postgres://fernscout:changeme@postgres:5432/fernscout

# Only used when you create the database by hand, to initialise
# database on first boot. Keep in sync with the credentials embedded in
# DATABASE_URL above.
# POSTGRES_USER=fernscout
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD=changeme
# POSTGRES_DB=fernscout

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Push notifications (features.push in site/config.json)
# Generate once with: npm run notify -- --generate-keys
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
# VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=
# VAPID_SUBJECT=mailto:you@example.com

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Auth / contacts (features.auth, features.contacts) — arrive with W08/W10,
# both need DATABASE_URL set as well.
#
# CONTACTS_ENCRYPTION_KEY encrypts readers' postal addresses at rest
# (AES-256-GCM) and derives their unsubscribe links. 32 bytes, as 64 hex
# characters or base64:
#
#   openssl rand -hex 32
#
# Keep it out of content/ — that folder is committed. Losing it makes every
# stored address unreadable and every unsubscribe link stop working; changing
# it does the same, so treat it as permanent.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SESSION_SECRET=
# CONTACTS_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# Fixes the six-digit sign-in code instead of generating one, so an
# end-to-end test can sign in without reading a mailbox. Without it the code is
# random and — with mail on `file` or `console` — printed where you can find
# it, which is what local development normally wants.
#
# **Never set this on anything reachable from the internet.** It turns the one
# credential this site has into a constant: anybody who knows the value can
# sign in as any address that may request a code.
# AUTH_DEV_CODE=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Mail (features.mail.transport) — "file" and "console" need nothing below;
# only "smtp" does.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SMTP_HOST=
# SMTP_PORT=587
# SMTP_USER=
# SMTP_PASSWORD=
# MAIL_FROM="Fernscout <postbox@example.com>"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Digest (npm run digest) — quiet rules, ROADMAP D8.
#
# Readers have a language, not a timezone, and inventing a field nobody fills
# in would be worse than guessing: "de" and "hu" readers are taken to be in
# CET/CEST, and everyone else — English is spoken in every timezone there is —
# is assumed to be near whoever runs the journal. This is that assumption.
# Defaults to Europe/Zurich.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DIGEST_TIMEZONE=Europe/Zurich

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WhatsApp (features.whatsapp.backend) — "dry-run" needs nothing below and
# writes the payload it would have sent to content/<user>/whatsapp/, which is
# how the whole feature is developed without a Meta account. "cloud" needs
# both of these.
#
# The token is a bearer credential for an account that can message real
# people, so it lives here and never in site/config.json — that file is
# committed. The dashboard's token expires in 24 hours; the one to deploy is a
# System User token from Meta Business Settings, which does not.
#
# WHATSAPP_PHONE_NUMBER_ID is the *phone number's* id, not the WhatsApp
# Business Account id — the two sit next to each other in the API Setup panel
# and only this one addresses messages.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHATSAPP_ACCESS_TOKEN=
# WHATSAPP_PHONE_NUMBER_ID=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Postcards / photobook print providers (features.postcards.provider,
# features.photobook.provider) — "dry-run" needs nothing below.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# STANNP_API_KEY=
# SWISSPOST_USERNAME=
# SWISSPOST_PASSWORD=
# PEECHO_API_KEY=
# GELATO_API_KEY=
# CLOUDPRINTER_API_KEY=
# Lulu prints photobooks and is the one provider here that wants a pair.
# LULU_CLIENT_KEY=
# LULU_CLIENT_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Address lookup (features.addressLookup) — turns coordinates into a place
# name. The default provider is Photon, which is public and needs no key; a
# provider you point `features.addressLookup.url` at may want one.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ADDRESS_LOOKUP_API_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The web helper at /agent (features.helper) — B684.
#
# This is the key the model calls are billed to, and it is the operator's
# rather than any journal's: a person using the helper spends *your* account,
# which is why `features.helper` is an operator-only capability and why it
# refuses to switch on unless `features.credits` is on too. Every call is
# metered before it is made, so an unmetered instance would be one where the
# bill has nowhere to land.
#
# Without it the capability reports itself off at /api/health and /agent says
# so, rather than failing at the moment somebody has finished typing.
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Speech to text (features.transcription.backend) — B686. "dry-run" needs
# nothing below and returns a canned transcript, which is how the feature is
# developed with no account anywhere; "deepgram" needs the key.
#
# The audio itself is never written to disk on any backend — it arrives with
# one request, goes to the provider, and is dropped when the request ends.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DEEPGRAM_API_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Ingest (npm run ingest) — see docs/ingest.md
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where untouched originals are archived. Served derivatives are stripped of
# all metadata, so this is the only copy that still carries GPS — which is
# exactly why it must live OUTSIDE content/, where no route can reach it.
# Unset means originals stay wherever you already keep them and only
# derivatives are written.
# MEDIA_ORIGINALS_DIR=/srv/fernscout-originals

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Backups (scripts/backup.sh) — an explicit allowlist (B653): the database
# (a pg_dump, or the sqlite file itself), content/, $DATA_DIR/config.json,
# the JSON stores lib/store.ts writes there, and the service's own env file
# minus RESTIC_PASSWORD — pushed off-VPS with restic. See docs/runbook.md.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A restic repository URL — a local path works for testing, but the whole
# point is that this points somewhere that is NOT this VPS: an S3-compatible
# bucket (Backblaze B2, Hetzner Object Storage, Cloudflare R2 via its S3 API),
# sftp://, or rest-server. See https://restic.readthedocs.io/en/stable/030_preparing_a_new_repo.html
# RESTIC_REPOSITORY=s3:https://s3.eu-central-003.backblazeb2.com/fernscout-backups
# RESTIC_PASSWORD=
# For S3-compatible backends:
# AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# How many nightly snapshots to keep before `restic forget --prune` trims them.
# BACKUP_KEEP_DAILY=14
#
# A second, off-site restic repository (B659): the machine taking the backup
# and the only copy of it must not be the same machine. Unset means one
# destination only, exactly as before. Encrypted with the SAME
# RESTIC_PASSWORD above — no second password to set. A different provider
# from RESTIC_REPOSITORY is the whole point (a second Backblaze bucket is not
# an off-site copy if the same account compromise reaches both). Prefer an
# append-only or write-only key for this one where the provider offers it:
# since B653 this env file travels inside the backup itself, so a key that can
# delete is a key that can delete the copy an attacker just found in it.
# RESTIC_REPOSITORY_SECONDARY=s3:https://<endpoint>/fernscout-backups-offsite
#
# The file staged as env/fernscout.env, RESTIC_PASSWORD stripped. Default
# /etc/fernscout/env, which is where systemd's EnvironmentFile= already points
# — override only for a non-systemd install.
# ENV_FILE=/etc/fernscout/env
#
# Whether a missing repository may be created. OFF, and meant to stay off: a
# typo in RESTIC_REPOSITORY otherwise becomes a brand new empty repository that
# gets backed into, pruned, and exits 0 — a green backup protecting nothing,
# while every real snapshot sits in the repository nobody writes to any more.
# Initialise once by hand (`restic init`), or set this for that one run.
# BACKUP_INIT_IF_MISSING=1
#
# Who is told how a backup run ended — scripts/alert.sh, started by the unit's
# OnFailure= and OnSuccess=. Needs mail to be switched on; without it the
# outcome is still recorded in $DATA_DIR and reported at /api/health.
#
# **Set this on any instance hosting journals other than your own.** Unset, a
# *failure* still goes to the default journal's owner.email, so a broken backup
# always reaches somebody. A *success* carries the instance status report —
# every journal by name, unlisted ones included, with its guests, credits and
# size — and that is withheld unless this variable names an operator, because
# owner.email comes out of a journal's own config file and the person who owns
# the default journal is not necessarily the person running the machine (B468).
# BACKUP_ALERT_EMAIL=
#
# How old the last successful backup may be before /api/health calls it stale.
# 36 hours by default: one missed nightly run is inside the window, two is not.
# BACKUP_MAX_AGE_HOURS=36

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Ops
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The one address that owns every journal on this instance (B480).
#
# Access here is per journal: a journal's own config.json names its owner, and
# nothing sits above that. On an instance where one person runs the machine —
# and where an agent works across several journals — that means no way in
# without being written into each config by hand. This is that way in.
#
# It is not a login of its own. The address still proves itself the ordinary
# way (a sign-in code, or an agent token), and what it then opens is everything
# a journal's owner opens, on every journal: drafts, private trips, publishing,
# the contacts page with its readers' postal addresses, invites, and credits.
# There is no read-only version of it.
#
# Here and not in site/config.json because it is an authorisation control:
# it changes on the server without a content commit, it does not travel to
# somebody else's instance in the repository, and no journal's own file can
# widen it. Unset — which is every instance that does not want this — nothing
# in the app behaves differently.
# FERNSCOUT_ADMIN_EMAIL=agent@example.com

# Set by CI/the deploy step (e.g. `export GIT_SHA=$(git rev-parse HEAD)` before
# `systemctl restart fernscout`) and read by the service at runtime;
# surfaced at /api/health so you can tell which build is actually running.
# GIT_SHA=

# Unlocks the *detail* on /api/health, which is unauthenticated and stays that
# way — an uptime monitor cannot hold a credential, so status, uptime, every
# block's ok/code and the whole capabilities block (including why each one is
# off) are public with or without this.
#
# What it unlocks is what an anonymous probe should not have: the absolute
# content-root path and errno text behind `content.error`, `config.error` and
# `basemap.error` (B234), and the whole `journals` block — every journal on
# this instance and which capabilities each one has switched on (B473).
#
# Without it that block is absent rather than empty, for anybody. A listed
# journal's *name* is public by design; its capability posture is a fact about
# a deployment, and the only reader who wants it is the operator comparing this
# page against a 404.
#
#   curl -sH "Authorization: Bearer $HEALTH_TOKEN" localhost:3000/api/health
#
# Unset entitles nobody, which is the state a fresh install wants. Generate one
# with `openssl rand -hex 32`.
# HEALTH_TOKEN=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Taking money for credits (B792).
#
# Both variables or neither. A secret key on its own would send a buyer to a
# real checkout page that no signature could confirm — money leaves, no credit
# lands — so /api/health reports the capability's provider as unconfigured
# until both are present, and purchases keep falling back to the operator
# approving them by hand (B425). That fallback is also what makes this
# developable with no Stripe account at all.
#
# **The key is the sandbox switch.** `sk_test_…` is Stripe's sandbox and moves
# no money; `sk_live_…` is real. There is deliberately no second flag saying
# which — a flag beside the key is a flag that can disagree with it, and the
# way that disagreement resolves is an instance charging real cards while every
# screen says "test". /api/health prints the mode it read from the prefix.
#
# A restricted key (`rk_…`) with write access to Checkout Sessions is enough.
#
# The webhook secret is the one Stripe shows when you add the endpoint
# https://<your-site>/api/webhooks/stripe for `checkout.session.completed`.
# Locally, `stripe listen --forward-to localhost:3000/api/webhooks/stripe`
# prints its own, which is different from the dashboard's.
#
# TWINT and cards (and therefore Apple Pay and Google Pay) are switched on at
# dashboard.stripe.com/settings/payment_methods, not here. TWINT is CHF-only,
# for customers in Switzerland, and capped at CHF 5000 per payment.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
